{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Which a person shall do and live by them.</b> It refers back to the משפטים, the ordinances, which are the life of man in the settling of lands, and they are the sustenance and enduring of the world, as it is written (Proverbs 29:4): ״מלך במשפט יעמיד ארץ״ — 'a king through justice establishes the land.'" "<b>And the received tradition</b> of our Rabbis, of blessed memory: ״וחי בהם״ — 'and live by them' — and not die by them. For the commandments are set aside for the preservation of life, and concerning all of them the halakhah was ruled: transgress and do not be killed, except for three, and they are: idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, and bloodshed, concerning which they said: be killed and do not transgress. And concerning all the other commandments, where they said transgress and do not be killed — that is specifically in private; but in public, even for a minor commandment, one is killed and does not transgress. Thus they concluded in the Gemara." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״וחי בהם״ — for the World to Come. And if you say, in this world — but his end is that he dies. And so Onkelos translated: ״וחי בהון חיי עלמא״ — 'and live by them in the life of the world.' Now the kinds of life, for body and for soul, through the performance of the commandments, are divided into four parts, and the word ״וחי״ embraces all four of them. <b>The first:</b> one who directs his intention in the performance of the commandments and does them on condition of receiving reward, and walks in them by way of the left, so that through them he may have his reward — wealth and possessions and honor, so that he may live many days; and this is what is written (Proverbs 3:16): ״בשמאלה עושר וכבוד״ — 'in her left hand, riches and honor'; and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, explained: for those who turn leftward (משמאילים) in her — wealth and possessions and honor. <b>The second:</b> one who directs his intention in them in order to take his reward in the World to Come, and even though he strives in their fulfillment and does them not for their own sake (שלא לשמה), on condition of receiving reward in the World to Come — nevertheless his soul merits to be there; and concerning these two kinds the prophet said (Isaiah 57:15): ״להחיות רוח שפלים ולהחיות לב נדכאים״ — 'to revive the spirit of the lowly and to revive the heart of the crushed.' <b>The third:</b> one who directs his intention in them to perform them out of love, as is fitting and proper, not on condition of receiving reward, while engaged in the affairs of this world — this one merits the life of this world and the life of the World to Come, like Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, who served out of love while engaged in the affairs of this world, in the sowing of the land and also the tending of livestock and other physical pursuits. <b>The fourth:</b> one who strives in the performance of the commandments and does not engage in any physical pursuit, as though he had no body at all, but his heart and thought are on the Holy One, blessed be He — behold, he merits enduring life forever, and death has no dominion over him at all, but he returns divine (אלהי) in body and in soul, like Enoch and Elijah, who were taken from the world in body and in soul." "<b>We further find</b> four kinds of life that a person merits through fulfillment of the Torah and the commandments. <b>The first:</b> life after the manner of human beings, which is life through the passage of time, and their soul persists in their body through the continuity of their health. <b>The second:</b> life after illness and after reaching the crisis of death — and this kind is a renewal of life renewed, like the likeness of an old and worn vessel that was renewed and returned to its beginning, in the manner of what is written (Job 33:25): ״רוטפש בשרו מנוער ישוב לימי עלומיו״ — 'his flesh becomes fresher than in youth; he returns to the days of his prime.' <b>The third:</b> life of forgiveness, after having been wicked — for he is considered as dead, and once he returns in repentance, behold he is alive, in the manner of what is written (Ezekiel 33:11): ״אם אחפוץ במות הרשע כי אם בשוב רשע מדרכו וחיה״ — 'Do I desire the death of the wicked? Rather, that the wicked turn back from his way and live.' <b>The fourth:</b> life after death, which is the resurrection of the dead. And these four kinds of life are all embraced in the word ״וחי בהםם״ — 'and live by them.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:28.334822669Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}